      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The American‑Hawaiian Steamship Company bought a 12,000‑acre ranch in 1963 and commissioned a master plan for a “City in the country.”.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.wlv.org/116/City-History",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The city’s roots trace back to the Chumash village of Hipuc, settled around 500 BCE.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Westlake_Village%2C_California",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Westlake Village’s Russell Ranch hosted filming of classic Westerns like Robin Hood (1922) and Bonanza (1959‑73).",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Westlake_Village%2C_California",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The community straddles the line between Los Angeles and Ventura counties, with roughly two‑thirds lying in Ventura’s Thousand Oaks.",
